Confusing gauge pressure with absolute pressure is the most frequent error in thermodynamics and boiler problems. Mixing units is next, especially bar and pascal, and kilojoule per hour and kilowatt.

Other common errors are:
confusing indicated, brake and shaft power;
confusing thermal efficiency with specific fuel consumption;
memorising a diagram without being able to say why each component is there;
forgetting safety devices such as relief valves, alarms and interlocks;
ignoring the legal and environmental side, which is examined and matters daily at sea;
accepting software output because it looks precise.

State your assumptions. An examiner, like a senior engineer, needs to see how you reached the answer as well as the answer itself.
